        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            No, not over loose joint sand. Polymeric sand needs a continuous inch to inch and a half of its own depth to grip the paver faces, and a shallow layer swept over compacted old sand cures into a brittle cap on a base that moves. It cracks, water gets under it, and the loose sand beneath washes out through the cracks — usually inside a season.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The exception is worth knowing, because it saves a lot of work when it applies: sound, cured polymeric sand that has simply eroded down is a perfectly good base, and fresh polymeric sand bonds to it. Test with a screwdriver — firm means top up, crumbly means remove.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When removal is needed, plan it as two days rather than one. Take the old sand out, deal with any weed roots at the same time, then let the joints dry completely before installing. Removing by pressure washer and refilling the same afternoon installs into damp joints and produces exactly the curing failure the whole exercise was meant to prevent.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy every bag in one purchase while you are at it. Polymeric sand is pigmented and batches vary slightly, so joints filled after a mid-project trip to the store can end up visibly a different tone from the ones you did first.
          </p>
